{"resourceIdentifier":"1532","resourceName":"COMACO Landscape Management Project","description":"The COMACO Landscape Management Project (CLMP) promotes sustainable agriculture and forest conservation. The main objectives of the project are twofold: 1) to sustainably increase smallholder farmer agricultural productivity, income, and welfare; and 2) to reduce uncontrolled forest loss and degradation and increase net forest cover. Hence, the project comprises two components:\r\n1. Sustainable Agricultural Land Management (SALM) component – which aims to promote widespread adoption of agricultural practices including conservation agriculture that increase food production per unit area and farmers’ income. The project promotes the adoption by farmers of legume-based alley cropping, residues management (e.g., mulching; cessation of residue burning), and reduced tillage among other SALM practices.\r\n2. REDD+ (Avoided Unplanned Deforestation) component – which aims to reduce forest loss, and protect and expand areas under natural forest, and conserve biodiversity. This is achieved primarily through land use planning and creation of Community Conservation Areas (CCA), coupled with sustainable non-extractive forest use, e.g., honey and mushrooms. \r\nThe project stretches across twenty-eight chiefdoms in eight districts in the Eastern Province of Zambia, namely: Chikomeni, Chikuwe, Jumbe, Luembe, Magodi, Mwasemphangwe, Mwape, Nyamphande, Zumwanda, Nyalugwe, Chinunda, Ndake, Mumbi, Mwanjawuntu, Kalindawalo, Nyanje, Katumba, Sandwe, Msoro, Mbangombe, Kawaza, Nzamane, Mpezeni, Mnukwa, Mafuta, Chanje, Kazembe and Madzimawe. It is a grouped project as defined in the VCS Standard (version 4.0); the first project activity instance comprises 7,372 ha of agricultural land for the SALM component, and 116,629 ha for the REDD+ component. The project has added 19 Chiefdoms in its third monitoring period increasing the project areas for both components to 44,944ha for SALM and 216,157ha for REDD+ component. The potential project area for the SALM component is estimated to be about 160,000 ha of agricultural land, and for the REDD+ at 757,023 ha of forest. \r\nPrior to the project, poor farming practices such as residue burning led to rapid soil nutrient depletion. There was also a general lack of land use planning and forest management, undertaken in a participatory and coordinated manner – which led to destruction and fragmentation of the forests. The COMACO Landscape Management Project uses market incentives to promote adoption of SALM practices and forest and wildlife conservation. It uses land use planning with traditional leaders and communities to identify and zone areas for conservation and forest management known as Community Conservation Areas (CCA). A Community Conservation Plan (CCP) is developed and implemented for specific CCAs – including activities for sustainable forest management, and rules regarding forest management. These activities will result in increased and sustainable agricultural production, and forest conservation. GHG emission reductions will be generated through improved carbon storage on farms, and avoidance of mosaic deforestation from unplanned agricultural land conversion. Two VCS-approved carbon methodologies are used to account for the project’s emissions reductions.\r\nThe annual average GHG emission reductions of the project are estimated at 237,841tCO2-e, and total GHG emission reductions over 20 years are estimated at 4,756,828 tCO2-e. This estimate considers only the first 10 years of GHG emission reductions for the REDD+ component.","location":{"latitude":-11.925421,"longitude":33.185848},"inPublicCommentPeriod":false,"lastPublicCommentPeriod":null,"attributes":[{"code":"PROJECT_ID","values":[{"type":"string","value":"1532"}]},{"code":"STATE_PROVINCE","values":[{"type":"string","value":"Eastern Province"}]}],"participationSummaries":[{"programCode":"VCS","attributes":[{"code":"PROPONENT_NAME","values":[{"typ